WEEK 1
Today, we did the following:

Downloaded the Arduino IDE (latest version: 2.3.10).
When you plug in the cable to the main component (the one with the little drawing on it), you should see some lights turn on on your Arduino.
Upon opening the software, a screen appears with a new "sketch". It contains some code, do not delete it.
`void setup()`: This is where you place your commands.
`void loop()`: This is where the main code goes, it contains the instructions you want to repeat continuously.
`void`: This indicates that something is absent. When placed before a function, it means the function does not return any value (this avoids the need for a `return` statement).
Saving a sketch: By default, everything is saved into a folder that is automatically created by the Arduino software. You must name this folder the same as the name you’ve given your sketch, otherwise, you will encounter an error.
Connecting the hardware to the code: Select "Board" (Arduino Uno). The output area allows you to view data regarding the component.
Next, select the "Port" (choose the one labeled "Arduino Uno"). It is important to select the correct one.
Once everything is properly configured, you can click "Upload" (the arrow icon next to the checkmark).
Arduino uses two types of pins: output and input.
For `digitalWrite`, you can specify either `HIGH` or `LOW`; this controls the voltage.
To alternate between `HIGH` and `LOW`, you need to add a `delay` to create dynamic behavior; for it to work correctly, the sequence must be included twice.

